Because the diagnosis pinpoints normalization as the cause, the fix principle is direct. \emph{Raw-Routed Mixture of Adapters} (RR-MoA) routes on the raw, pre-normalization input $\mathbf{X}_\text{raw}$ while keeping the backbone strictly frozen; only the router and five canonical expert heads are trainable. The same principle predicts a \emph{Frozen Paradox}: unfreezing triggers co-adaptation collapse (Proposition~\ref{prop:frozen}), so within a fixed MoE architecture frozen should match or beat unfrozen, which we confirm empirically (frozen best on $4/6$, unfrozen-wins bounded at $\leq 13\%$).

\paragraph{Contributions.}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.2em, itemsep=0.15em, topsep=0.2em]
\item We identify \emph{normalization-induced routing collapse}, a failure mode of MoE adapters on TSFMs that is distinct from the optimization-induced collapse studied in standard MoE-rescue literature, and isolate it through eight causal controls that converge on normalization as the cause.
\item We formalize the mechanism through a mutual-information decomposition (Observation~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}) that yields a signal-ratio predictor of which datasets benefit from raw routing, including boundary cases where they do not, and validate the same principle across three normalization families, five backbones, and a vision modality.
\item We propose RR-MoA, the \emph{minimal causal intervention} that fixes the collapse: changing only the router's input, with no other change to the optimizer, the experts, or the backbone. Larger optimization-side interventions fail ($720$ runs, $\leq 10.9\%$ recovery, $2.7{\times}$ worse than RR-MoA), naive $(\mu,\sigma)$ re-injection fails ($+42$--$239\%$ MSE; $11/18$ cells re-collapse), and the signal-ratio predictor correctly flags datasets where raw routing should \emph{not} help (Traffic, $R{=}0.14$). A companion variant (Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+}) extends the same principle to the expert level; together they close the gap to per-dataset supervised baselines while preserving frozen-backbone deployability.
\end{itemize}

